Understanding information diffusion in microblogging platforms is important for discourse quality, yet there are no established ways to do it without humans, in a safe and reproducible environment. This thesis asks whether a minimal set of mechanics of a microblogging social network ---an asymmetric follow graph, a reverse-chronological timeline and repost amplification, driven by the users' own activity rhythms--- is sufficient to reproduce the underlying dynamics of diffusion. To answer it, a high-performance Discrete-Event Simulation (DES) with Event-Scheduling engine, implemented from scratch in Zig, formalizes the platform as a Time-Varying Heterogeneous Graph and assembles multiple Continuous-Time Independent Cascade (CTIC) models as information cascades with activity-driven user sessions. The simulation makes user session starts and ends, post creations and interactions serviced by reverse-chronological timeline queues. The model is calibrated end to end on telemetry extracted from the open Bluesky Firehose ---per-user session, posting and interaction distributions, and a forest-fire-sampled follower topology. Executed on up to one million users, the engine scales slightly super-linearly and reaches a stationary regime. The calibrated core reproduces only the shape class of the empirical cascades ---tiny, shallow and broadcast-dominated, with the same lognormal repost distribution--- not their magnitudes: size, width and mean depth sit below the data, the non-trivial-cascade rate is roughly half the empirical one, Structural Virality stays below the broadcast floor, and the heavy tail is truncated; the reasons are located rather than residual. Depth is capped by the subcritical reproduction number that content-agnostic behaviour imposes on every cascade, and width by the impression budget and, most strongly, an inflated post arrival rate ---not by reverse-chronological order, which the simulation tests and rejects as a cap; the two truncations are coupled, since the arrival rate also depresses the reproduction number. Together with in-network-only impressions and a uniform conversion rate, these caps specify the mechanism the model lacks: post-level heterogeneity and out-of-network impressions, i.e. content-aware behaviour and recommendation.

*Keywords*: Information Diffusion, Discrete-Event Simulation (DES), Continuous-Time Independent Cascade (CTIC), Bluesky/AT Protocol, Activity-Driven Networks, Branching Processes, Structural Virality
